Z||000008||80's BOY||09-21-2000||11:58 AM||||hey guys i started building the panel for the free-air subs behind the seat headrests to cover the rear glass. hey seanh, sounds like you know alot more about stereos than turbos, so here's a question. should i port the box for these free-airs or do you think the "leakage" from a non-sealed system would be enough. this is mostly for looks, but i still want it to sound good. large ports or small?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Seanh||09-21-2000||06:06 PM||||hmm. Im not sure what your asking 80's boy. If you are wonding about sealing and porting the rear chamber (back of the subs) and you are using free-airs. Then No, I would not port them. I really dont work all that much with free-airs. My idea of your idea is still cloudy. I would like to know more about this. If your doing what I think your doing, its not going to sound very good. If I were you, Id build a sealed box that fits in the rear window area and uses around .4-.6 cubic ft. of airspace (per sub) in a stereo 2nd order bandbass design. Just a thought. I really havent looked into putting subs in a rear window like that. You guys know if you really wanted a bass machine in a Fiero you could put 2 12's behind each seat, there for having 4 12's behind the seats, 2 10's or 12's (havent looked into this) in the rear window, 2 18's in the trunk and ported into the car, 1 8" in the factory location, and 2 10's up front. That would be wicked. Probally have to have ear plugs and a good suspension system. Just a thought to ponder.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||09-21-2000||06:09 PM||||I just took a look at your message again and noticed somthing. I think I get the idea. And yes, I would port the box, probally 2 possibly 2-4" ports. What size subs are you going to use?||||reg||1
Z||000011||80's BOY||09-21-2000||08:26 PM||||i've going with 12's. i am also going to use 1/2" padding with white vinyl overlay for a "snug" fit. picture looking into the fiero and seeing two 12's with a walled out design behind the back seats. even though the padding will snug it up a bit, there will probably still be leakage right? i've never been very good at porting boxes. tried it once, but went back to sealed.||||reg||14
Z||000012||Seanh||09-21-2000||09:04 PM||||Yeah, there will be some leakage, but I think it should be okay. Probally should just stick with sealed. Possible look at spraying the inside with sound dampening spray before putting the matting in it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||pHoOl||09-22-2000||01:02 AM||||I read of people putting 12s behind the seats, and i have to wonder: how tall are they?  I'm 6'2", and i want some bass, but i need every inch i can get in seat travel.  moving it up even three inches would not be desirable<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||09-22-2000||07:33 AM||||The way I put them in, you dont have to move the seat at all. Once installed then dont go back as far, but that is hardly a problem and barly noticable. For anyone interested this will be available in mid october.||||reg||1
Z||000015||whamel||09-22-2000||09:43 AM||||Hey - I just think it's pretty cool that we are getting some positive press here.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As far as my system, I'm still searching for a head unit to use right now, but I have the 4x10/4x6 thing going (Pioneer/Blaupunkt) as well as two pair of JBL 5 1/4's that I"m going to put in the door and tranny hump.  I've got a pair of 8" subs for behind the seat too.  Shouldn't sound too terribly bad when I get done.||||reg||1
